BODY = '''<h2>What the Federal Rule and the CDC Say</h2>
<p>A powered scooter is a regulated medical device. Under 21 CFR 890.3800, a motorized three-wheeled vehicle is a "gasoline-fueled or battery-powered device intended for medical purposes", used for outside transportation by disabled persons, and the rule classifies it as Class II. That is the class the Rascal listing names. The page we read does not say whether the device is exempt from premarket notification, and we could not read the FDA's own page on scooters, so we make no claim about any specific scooter's clearance.</p>
<p>The CDC says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, that fewer than half tell their doctor, and that a single fall doubles the chance of another. A scooter can help you travel without a long walk, but you still have to get on and off it, and you or a helper has to lift its heaviest piece into a car.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>Lift the heaviest piece before you buy.</strong> The listings give weights of 27 to 29.5 pounds for the heaviest piece on the three that state it, and 30 pounds for the Iris 3 with the seat and batteries removed. Try that weight, and ask a doctor or occupational therapist whether a scooter suits you.</p>

<h2>Mobility Scooter Comparison: The Five Side by Side</h2>
<p>Every figure is from the Amazon listing, checked on 2026-10-20. A dash means the listing text we read does not give it. The heaviest piece is what someone must lift into a car.</p>
<table class="art-table"><thead><tr><th>Scooter</th><th>Weight limit</th><th>Range</th><th>Top speed</th><th>Heaviest piece</th><th>Tires</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Vive 3 Wheel</td><td>265 lb</td><td>Up to 12.4 miles</td><td>3.7 mph</td><td>29 lb (rear section)</td><td>8 in flat-free</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rascal Venture</td><td>300 lb</td><td>Up to 10 miles</td><td>Up to 4 mph</td><td>29.5 lb</td><td>&ndash;</td></tr>
<tr><td>SuperHandy Passport</td><td>275 lb</td><td>Up to 13 miles (two batteries)</td><td>3.7 mph</td><td>27 lb (frame)</td><td>6.75 in solid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hoverfly T3</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>Up to 13 miles</td><td>3.7 mph (1.9 reverse)</td><td>&ndash; (61 lb in total)</td><td>8 in shock-absorbing solid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Iris 3</td><td>265 lb including cargo</td><td>20 miles (8Ah) or 32 (12Ah)</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>30 lb (seat and batteries off)</td><td>&ndash;</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The Iris 3 is the lightest to lift and goes farthest, the Rascal takes the most weight, and the Vive and Hoverfly have the largest tires. Real range is shorter on hills, and no listing gives its test conditions.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Mobility Scooter</h2>
<h3>Weight of the heaviest piece</h3>
<p>Someone must lift it into a car. The listings say 27 pounds for the SuperHandy frame, 29 for the Vive rear section and 29.5 for the Rascal, and the Iris 3 lifts at 30 with the seat and batteries off.</p>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>The limits are 265 pounds for the Vive, 275 for the SuperHandy, 265 including cargo for the Iris 3 and 300 for the Rascal. The Hoverfly listing text gives none.</p>
<h3>Range and speed</h3>
<p>Listed ranges run from 10 miles on the Rascal to 32 on the Iris 3 with its larger batteries. Speeds are 3.7 to 4 mph. Range falls with hills, weight and cold, and the listings do not give the test conditions.</p>
<h3>Tires</h3>
<p>The Vive and Hoverfly have 8-inch tires, and the SuperHandy has 6.75-inch solid ones that the listing says suit flat surfaces. Larger tires roll over cracks and thresholds more easily.</p>
<h3>Seat and getting on and off</h3>
<p>The Vive and Rascal list a swivel seat, and the Vive has armrests that flip up. The SuperHandy has no armrests, per its listing.</p>
<h3>Batteries and travel</h3>
<p>The Passport and Iris 3 use removable lithium batteries. Airlines have rules for lithium batteries, and we could not read the FAA's page, so ask your airline before you fly.</p>

<h2>Mobility Scooter Questions</h2>
<h3>Do I need a prescription for a mobility scooter?</h3>
<p>Not to buy one from Amazon, as far as the listings show. If you want a plan or Medicare to pay, ask your doctor and the plan first. Medicare.gov's page on durable medical equipment says that suppliers must be enrolled and that a provider must order the equipment.</p>
<h3>Can I take a scooter on a plane?</h3>
<p>The Passport and Iris 3 listings describe airline-friendly batteries, but those are seller claims, and we could not read the FAA's page on mobility devices. Airlines have their own rules for lithium batteries and for how a scooter is checked in, so call the airline before you book and ask what they need, such as the battery watt-hours and whether it must come out of the scooter.</p>
<h3>Is a scooter safer than walking?</h3>
<p>It depends. A scooter lets you travel without a long walk, but the CDC's fall figures apply to getting on and off, and you can tip over on slopes. Ask a doctor or occupational therapist.</p>
<h3>How far will it go?</h3>
<p>The listings say up to 10 miles for the Rascal, 12.4 for the Vive, 13 for the SuperHandy and Hoverfly, and 20 to 32 for the Iris 3. Real range is shorter on hills.</p>
<h3>What does FDA Class II mean for a scooter?</h3>
<p>Under 21 CFR 890.3800, a motorized three-wheeled vehicle is a battery-powered device intended for medical purposes, for outside transportation by disabled persons, and the rule classifies it as Class II (performance standards). The Rascal listing says its scooter is a Class II device. The rule text we read did not say whether a scooter is exempt from premarket notification, so it does not tell you that a given scooter was cleared.</p>
<h3>How long does a scooter take to charge?</h3>
<p>The listings vary. The SuperHandy Passport listing says its batteries recharge in 1.5 hours, and the Hoverfly listing says a 5-hour charge gives up to 13 miles. The Vive, Rascal and Iris listings in the text we read give no charge time. Ask the seller before you buy, and plan for the longer figure if you ride every day.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Vive is the best pick for most people. It has a swivel seat, flip-up armrests and 8-inch flat-free tires, and 1,146 reviews average 4.3 stars. Its heaviest piece is 29 pounds and the limit is 265, and every figure comes from the listing.</p>
<p>The Rascal Venture is the choice for a heavier user, with a 300-pound rating, a 4.7 rating and a Class II label that matches the federal rule, but only 116 reviews and a 10-mile range. The SuperHandy Passport has the lightest frame at 27 pounds and two batteries, but no armrests and 6.75-inch solid tires for flat surfaces.</p>
<p>The Hoverfly is the cheapest, with only 24 reviews and no weight limit in the listing text. The Iris 3 is the lightest to lift and has the longest range, but it is the most expensive and has only 13 reviews. Check with the seller about the limit on each before you order.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Vive 3 Wheel is the best all-round portable scooter, the Rascal Venture the choice for 300 pounds and the SuperHandy Passport the budget travel pick. Lift the heaviest piece first, ask your airline about the batteries and check with a doctor or occupational therapist that a scooter suits you. See also our guides to <a href="/best-rollators/">rollators</a> and <a href="/best-transport-wheelchairs/">transport wheelchairs</a>.</p>'''

